A||||4||XDrewX||Clutch Question: Is there adjustment ??||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||10-06-2001||09:47 PM||||O.K.  It has been about 15 years since i drove a stick.<P>I test drove a fiero today, that I may buy for my son.<P>In order to shift gears,  the clutch had to be planted to the floor.<P>The last stick I owned, just the weight of your foot was enough to let you shift gears.<BR>I drove that car hard and never had the clutch slip.<P>Is there a way to adjust the travel of the clutch ???<P>Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-06-2001||10:48 PM||||Nope.  There is no adjustment for the clutch.  The Fiero uses a hydraulic clutch system that self adjusts.<P>If the clutch engages all the way at the floor, then there is a hydraulic problem......either the master cylinder or slave cylinder are probably shot.  A bent clutch pedal also creates similar problems.  Of course, the clutch itself could be screwed up too.....that happens with continued use after one of the hydraulic components fail.<P>Bottom line is you will need to do some clutch work.  Worst case scenario is a new clutch kit, new master, new slave & new pedal......these parts will total about $400-$500 + installation (add at least another $500).  If you do it yourself it'll be cheaper, but a general PITA to do.<P>My feeling is this......unless the car is in great shape and you'd be getting a super deal on it, don't buy it.  Wait until one that doesn't need any clutch work done comes along....you'll be happier in the long run.<P>BTW.....only buy/install genuine GM hydraulics.  That is the slave and master cylinders.......the aftermarket stuff sucks.<P>Good luck!  <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTMike||10-06-2001||11:11 PM||||or it could be just low  on fluid...meaning you hvae to push the pedal further to move the little bit thats in there. check the level if its low thats the problem.  my dads mr2 had this problem and all i did was fill it up and bleed it. took me 20 min. and its good as new. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||frankshel||10-07-2001||12:41 AM||||I have told people before...unhook the clutch rod from the pedal. Cut the rod in two with a hack saw blade. Take a piece of steel, either round or square about 1" in diameter and 3-4" long. Drill a hole in each end with a drill bit the size of the pedal rod leaving 1/2" of material between the holes which will give you more than enough for the clutch to disengage. Mine has been in for 7 years and I have not had a problem. The throw out bearing won't rub..trust me...I have done this for 6 other people and they have not had a problem. Sometimes, a new clutch cylinder is not the answer. I own 5 Fieros ..3 are standards and they all have this retro-fit. Call me at work during the day at  518-273-4110  ext 310  ask for Frank Sheldon and I can better explain it. PS&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;this will save you a lot of money and it works. Good Luck,<BR>Frank||||reg||1
Z||000004||frankshel||10-07-2001||12:44 AM||||PS.....Slide the spacer back in between the two pieces of the cut rod and reattach it to the pedal. It won't come apart. You can also fill the holes with epoxy if you are worried about it. Frank Sheldon||||reg||1
